tent": "Does the normal creatinine and absence of polyuria/polydipsia contradict endocrine hypercalcemia?", "summary": "Normal renal function and lack of polyuria/polydipsia make endocrine causes like hyperparathyroidism less likely."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Malignancy with Hypercalcemia and Elevated ALP? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "summary": "No single finding in the vignette strongly contradicts malignancy. Constitutional symptoms and ALP elevation remain consistent with this branch."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B3", 0.615], ["B3.1", 0.274], ["B1", 0.237]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No universal instability markers (airway, respiratory compromise, shock, altered mental status, uncontrolled hemorrhage) are present. Time-critical syndromes (STEMI, stroke, septic shock) are not indicated by the current data.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading hypothesis (B3) has posterior <0.75 and unresolved dangerous alternatives (B3.1, B3.2, B3.3) with post >=0.10 and danger >=0.7. Tree depth (2) < max allowed (3)."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.32, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.2735042735042735, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely explanation of this patient's current presentation?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Antacid overuse"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Increased 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Increased parathyroid hormone"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Malignancy"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Viral illness"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'malignancy', 'hypercalcemia'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B3", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.615}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B3.1", "label": "Solid Tumor with Bone Metastases (e.g., Breast, Lung, Prostate)", "posterior": 0.274, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1", "B3.2", "B3.3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=20%, source=dxs]\n\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.65):]\n  Source: Hypercalcemia of Malignancy. > Deterrence and Patient Education\n  Patients with calcium abnormalities related to underlying cancer should follow up with an endocrinologist in addition to receiving mandatory oncology evaluation and treatment. If complications arise i\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.65):]\n  Source: Update on multiple endocrine neoplasia Type 1 and 2. > History and Physical\n  It is also important to assess patients for hypercalcemia symptoms due to primary hyperparathyroidism and its associated clinical complications (eg, renal calculi and osteoporosis); signs and symptoms\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.86):]\n  Source: Update on multiple endocrine neoplasia Type 1 and 2. > History and Physical\n  It is also important to assess patients for hypercalcemia symptoms due to primary hyperparathyroidism and its associated clinical complications (eg, renal calculi and osteoporosis); signs and symptoms\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.87):]\n  Source: Metastatic bone disease: diagnosis, evaluation, and treatment. > History and Physical\n  Pathologic fractures can be preceded by lesions producing prodromal pain or can be indolent until the time of fracture. Patients may or may not report B symptoms, including unintentional weight loss, \n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  poorly controlled GERD with antacid use → excessive calcium intake from antacids → Endocrine Hypercalcemia\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Assess calcium levels and antacid usage patterns to determine if hypercalcemia is secondary to over-the-counter medication use\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
